WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.380
Data management begins with a model, not a
query.

2
00:00:03.380 --> 00:00:06.568
A relation is commonly presented as a table.

3
00:00:06.568 --> 00:00:13.245
Columns represent attributes with defined
domains, and rows represent tuples describing

4
00:00:13.245 --> 00:00:13.852
instances.

5
00:00:13.852 --> 00:00:20.118
A students table might contain a student
identifier and name; an enrollments table can

6
00:00:20.118 --> 00:00:23.698
represent the relationship between students
and course sections.

7
00:00:23.698 --> 00:00:29.132
Every column needs one meaning, one unit, and
an appropriate type.

8
00:00:29.132 --> 00:00:34.515
Avoid storing several values in one cell or
repeating groups of columns.

9
00:00:34.515 --> 00:00:39.056
A schema is a contract about the shape and
interpretation of stored data.

10
00:00:39.056 --> 00:00:45.025
If the meaning is ambiguous, technically valid
SQL can still produce meaningless results.

11
00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:50.872
A key is an attribute or minimal set of
attributes that uniquely identifies a row.

12
00:00:50.872 --> 00:00:56.561
A table may have several candidate keys; one
is selected as the primary key.

13
00:00:56.561 --> 00:01:03.476
Stable generated identifiers are often useful,
but a generated key does not remove the need

14
00:01:03.476 --> 00:01:06.242
to enforce real-world uniqueness when
required.

15
00:01:06.242 --> 00:01:12.891
A foreign key stores a value that must match a
referenced key, representing a declared

16
00:01:12.891 --> 00:01:13.334
relationship.

17
00:01:13.334 --> 00:01:17.173
An enrollment can reference one student and
one section.

18
00:01:17.173 --> 00:01:21.731
Foreign-key constraints prevent orphaned
references, but designers must choose what

19
00:01:21.731 --> 00:01:25.834
should happen when referenced data changes or
is deleted.

20
00:01:25.834 --> 00:01:32.467
Keys give relationships executable structure
rather than leaving them as comments or naming

21
00:01:32.467 --> 00:01:32.977
conventions.

22
00:01:33.098 --> 00:01:36.759
Types define which values a column can
represent.

23
00:01:36.759 --> 00:01:41.479
Constraints narrow those values to the valid
states of the application.

24
00:01:41.479 --> 00:01:43.634
Not null requires a value.

25
00:01:43.634 --> 00:01:47.716
Check constraints express conditions such as a
nonnegative quantity.

26
00:01:47.716 --> 00:01:50.484
Unique constraints prevent duplicates.

27
00:01:50.484 --> 00:01:54.808
Primary and foreign keys enforce identity and
references.

28
00:01:54.808 --> 00:02:00.178
Put durable invariants in the database when
every application must obey them.

29
00:02:00.178 --> 00:02:06.092
Validation in a user interface improves
feedback, but it can be bypassed by another

30
00:02:06.092 --> 00:02:07.359
client or import.

31
00:02:07.359 --> 00:02:13.626
Constraints also need careful scope: a rule
involving current time or external state may

32
00:02:13.626 --> 00:02:16.759
not behave like a stable row invariant.

33
00:02:16.759 --> 00:02:22.027
Good constraints make invalid data difficult
to store and failures explicit.

34
00:02:22.154 --> 00:02:28.116
A basic select statement identifies source
tables, filters rows with a where condition,

35
00:02:28.116 --> 00:02:31.785
and projects the columns or expressions to
return.

36
00:02:31.785 --> 00:02:35.126
The result is itself a table-like relation.

37
00:02:35.126 --> 00:02:41.009
SQL describes the desired result more than a
step-by-step loop, allowing the database to

38
00:02:41.009 --> 00:02:42.690
choose an execution plan.

39
00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:48.360
Be precise about null: it represents missing
or unknown information and does not behave

40
00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:50.790
like an ordinary value in comparisons.

41
00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:53.838
Use IS NULL rather than equals null.

42
00:02:53.838 --> 00:02:58.073
Ordering is not guaranteed unless an ORDER BY
clause requests it.

43
00:02:58.073 --> 00:03:05.097
Select only needed columns, use meaningful
aliases, and verify the result on a tiny

44
00:03:05.097 --> 00:03:08.609
hand-checkable dataset before trusting a large
output.

45
00:03:08.738 --> 00:03:12.680
Joins reconstruct relationships stored across
tables.

46
00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:17.246
An inner join keeps combinations that satisfy
the join condition.

47
00:03:17.246 --> 00:03:23.273
A left outer join also keeps unmatched rows
from the left side, filling missing right-side

48
00:03:23.273 --> 00:03:24.478
values with nulls.

49
00:03:24.478 --> 00:03:31.814
Always identify the intended cardinality: one
to one, one to many, or many to many through

50
00:03:31.814 --> 00:03:33.190
an associative table.

51
00:03:33.190 --> 00:03:39.057
A missing predicate creates a Cartesian
product, while an incomplete predicate can

52
00:03:39.057 --> 00:03:40.524
silently duplicate results.

53
00:03:40.524 --> 00:03:45.830
Qualify ambiguous column names and inspect row
counts before and after the join.

54
00:03:45.830 --> 00:03:48.254
Ask what one result row represents.

55
00:03:48.254 --> 00:03:54.376
If that sentence is unclear, aggregation and
reporting built on the join will also be

56
00:03:54.376 --> 00:03:54.784
unclear.

57
00:03:54.914 --> 00:04:00.004
Count, sum, average, minimum, and maximum
summarize rows.

58
00:04:00.004 --> 00:04:03.358
Group by determines which rows share one
output group.

59
00:04:03.358 --> 00:04:08.269
If a query groups by course, each result row
represents one course.

60
00:04:08.269 --> 00:04:13.409
Columns in the select list must either define
that grain or be aggregated.

61
00:04:13.409 --> 00:04:19.927
Where filters individual rows before grouping;
having filters groups after aggregation.

62
00:04:19.927 --> 00:04:24.341
Null handling matters because many aggregates
ignore null values.

63
00:04:24.341 --> 00:04:30.284
Distinct may remove duplicates, but using it
to hide a faulty join masks the real problem.

64
00:04:30.284 --> 00:04:36.990
State the result grain in words, create a
small example, and verify that every join and

65
00:04:36.990 --> 00:04:39.085
group preserves the intended meaning.

66
00:04:39.218 --> 00:04:45.138
A poorly designed table may repeat the same
course title, instructor office, or department

67
00:04:45.138 --> 00:04:47.675
name across hundreds of enrollment rows.

68
00:04:47.675 --> 00:04:53.220
Repetition creates anomalies: one update can
leave conflicting versions, deleting the last

69
00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:59.228
enrollment can erase course information, and
inserting a course may require a fake

70
00:04:59.228 --> 00:04:59.690
enrollment.

71
00:04:59.690 --> 00:05:05.913
Normalization analyzes which attributes depend
on which keys and separates different kinds of

72
00:05:05.913 --> 00:05:07.828
facts into related tables.

73
00:05:07.828 --> 00:05:13.449
Decomposition should preserve the ability to
reconstruct necessary information through

74
00:05:13.449 --> 00:05:16.821
joins and enforce dependencies where possible.

75
00:05:16.821 --> 00:05:21.157
Normalization is not a command to maximize
table count.

76
00:05:21.157 --> 00:05:26.656
It is a reasoning tool for storing each
durable fact in a controlled place while

77
00:05:26.656 --> 00:05:28.122
keeping the model usable.

78
00:05:28.250 --> 00:05:34.255
An index is an auxiliary structure that helps
the database locate rows without scanning

79
00:05:34.255 --> 00:05:35.113
every row.

80
00:05:35.113 --> 00:05:41.031
A B-tree index often supports equality and
range conditions on its leading keys.

81
00:05:41.031 --> 00:05:44.373
Unique indexes can also enforce uniqueness.

82
00:05:44.373 --> 00:05:46.057
Indexes are not free.

83
00:05:46.057 --> 00:05:51.569
Inserts, updates, and deletes must maintain
them, they consume storage, and an index that

84
00:05:51.569 --> 00:05:55.113
does not match query predicates may not be
used.

85
00:05:55.113 --> 00:06:00.687
The query planner estimates costs and chooses
among scans and join strategies.

86
00:06:00.687 --> 00:06:06.432
Index columns based on important workload
patterns, then inspect actual plans and

87
00:06:06.432 --> 00:06:06.911
measurements.

88
00:06:06.911 --> 00:06:12.347
Adding an index to every column can make
writes slower without improving the queries

89
00:06:12.347 --> 00:06:13.123
that matter.

90
00:06:13.274 --> 00:06:18.887
Many business actions require several
statements to succeed as one unit.

91
00:06:18.887 --> 00:06:23.058
A transfer may subtract from one account and
add to another.

92
00:06:23.058 --> 00:06:28.998
If the process fails between those statements,
the database must not preserve half a

93
00:06:28.998 --> 00:06:29.422
transfer.

94
00:06:29.422 --> 00:06:35.290
A transaction begins a unit of work and ends
with commit to make changes durable or

95
00:06:35.290 --> 00:06:36.757
rollback to discard them.

96
00:06:36.757 --> 00:06:43.121
Concurrent transactions also need isolation
rules so interleaving operations do not

97
00:06:43.121 --> 00:06:44.856
produce invalid results.

98
00:06:44.856 --> 00:06:51.309
Keep transactions as short as practical,
handle failures explicitly, and do not assume

99
00:06:51.309 --> 00:06:56.272
that application code alone can restore
consistency after every crash.

100
00:06:56.272 --> 00:07:02.394
Transaction boundaries should match the atomic
action users and business rules recognize.

101
00:07:02.522 --> 00:07:06.515
A professional database design answers nine
questions.

102
00:07:06.515 --> 00:07:08.938
What does each table and row represent?

103
00:07:08.938 --> 00:07:12.637
Which keys identify rows and connect
relations?

104
00:07:12.637 --> 00:07:15.239
Which constraints prevent invalid states?

105
00:07:15.239 --> 00:07:18.097
What is the grain of each query result?

106
00:07:18.097 --> 00:07:21.744
Do join predicates match the intended
cardinality?

107
00:07:21.744 --> 00:07:24.589
Are repeated facts creating anomalies?

108
00:07:24.589 --> 00:07:27.484
Which workload justifies each index?

109
00:07:27.484 --> 00:07:29.780
Which changes must commit atomically?

110
00:07:29.780 --> 00:07:33.364
What do query plans and measurements show on
realistic data?

111
00:07:33.364 --> 00:07:36.489
SQL syntax is only the surface.

112
00:07:36.489 --> 00:07:41.948
Trustworthy data systems come from preserving
meaning and integrity while giving

113
00:07:41.948 --> 00:07:46.910
applications efficient, testable ways to
derive results and apply changes.

114
00:07:46.910 --> 00:07:52.964
Finish by testing the schema with a tiny
dataset that contains a valid relationship, a

115
00:07:52.964 --> 00:07:58.211
missing reference, a duplicate, a null, and a
transaction that must roll back.

116
00:07:58.211 --> 00:08:02.000
Then inspect the actual query plan for the
most important read.

117
00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:08.211
Those checks connect the conceptual model to
enforceable behavior and measured performance.
